From 8f0dcd9c7164f23253c0b6054c5966aa0bc18891 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ChenXiaoSong <chenxiaosong@kylinos.cn>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 16:06:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] smb/server: recognize stored symbolic link targets

Recognize user.ksmbd.symlink on regular files and directories as a stored
link target. Present its effective type and length consistently in
directory entries, POSIX CREATE contexts, QUERY_INFO and CLOSE replies.
Protect the private attribute from generic SMB EA reads and writes.

This supplies the read side before remote creation is enabled. A stored
link keeps its backing inode; local tools still see a regular file or
directory. Its xattr contains target bytes without a terminating NUL.

Example (use a fresh test share, set attributes before mounting):

  1. Server:
     printf 'target\n' > /export/file
     mkdir /export/subdir /export/stored-dir
     printf 'nested\n' > /export/subdir/file
     touch /export/stored
     setfattr -n user.ksmbd.symlink -v file /export/stored
     setfattr -n user.ksmbd.symlink -v subdir /export/stored-dir
  2. Client:
     for mode in noposix posix; do
         mount -t cifs //${server_ip}/test /mnt \
             -o username=${smb_user},vers=3.1.1,$mode || exit 1
         ls -l /mnt/stored /mnt/stored-dir
         readlink /mnt/stored
         stat -c %s /mnt/stored
         cat /mnt/stored
         cat /mnt/stored-dir/file
         umount /mnt || exit 1
     done

Expect symbolic links in ls, then file, 4, target and nested. A client
getfattr request must not expose user.ksmbd.symlink. Repeat queries after
remounting to check that the stored metadata survives handle closure.
The server-side setfattr commands above are test fixtures for this stage.

Code and test instructions generated with OpenAI Codex assistance.
